  "definition": {
   "definition": "The Cash Reserve Ratio is the share of a bank's net demand and time liabilities (aggregate deposits) that it must hold as a cash balance with the Reserve Bank. It is imposed under Section 42 of the Reserve Bank of India Act, 1934, and the operative rules are set out in the Master Direction — RBI (Cash Reserve Ratio and Statutory Liquidity Ratio) Directions, 2021, issued under Section 35A of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949 and pursuant to Section 42 of the RBI Act. RBI publishes the rate in force week by week in the WSS 'Ratios and Rates' table. A CRR cut releases lendable funds and raises the money multiplier; a hike absorbs them, so a change here is itself policy news.",
   "source_name": "RBI Master Direction — Cash Reserve Ratio and Statutory Liquidity Ratio Directions, 2021",
